---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import PhotoSlot from '../../components/PhotoSlot.astro';
import OnsitePhoto from '../../components/OnsitePhoto.astro';
import { waLink } from '../../data/site.ts';

const title = 'China trade shows and the factory visits that follow them';
const description =
  'How we pair a Chinese trade show with real factory visits: Yiwu Fair in October, the East China Fair in Shanghai, Canton Fair seasons, the Ningbo export hall in May, and what to verify on the floor afterwards.';

const wa = waLink('Hi, I am planning around a trade show in China and want to add factory visits.');
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="trade-shows"
  crumbs={[]}
  current="Trade shows"
  cta={{
    eyebrow: 'Next step',
    title: 'Going to a fair? Add the factory days.',
    lead: 'Tell us which fair and roughly when. We will say whether the factory days fit around it, or that the timing does not work.',
    primary: { label: 'Plan around a trade show', href: '/contact/' },
    secondary: { label: 'Ask on WhatsApp', href: wa, external: true },
  }}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="split-7-5">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Trade shows</p>
          <h1>A fair gets you samples. A factory gets you the truth.</h1>
          <p class="lead hero__lead">
            Sourcing platforms and fair organisers will happily tell you what a trade show is and when
            it runs — they do it better than we could, and there is no point competing with them. What
            nobody sells you is the part that follows: walking into the actual factory behind the
            booth, before you commit.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/contact/">Plan a fair week with factory days</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__media">
          <PhotoSlot name="TRADE SHOW · THEN FACTORY" variant="media">
            <OnsitePhoto id="08" eager />
          </PhotoSlot>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Section tight>
    <div class="container">
      <SectionHead
        title="The fairs we plan around"
        lead="We only list fairs we can actually combine with factory visits inside the same trip. Dates are set by each organiser and do move — confirm on the official site before booking anything."
      />
      <ul class="spec-list">
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">Yiwu Fair · October · Yiwu</span>
          <span
            >The China Yiwu International Commodities Fair runs every October at the Yiwu International
            Expo Centre — the 32nd edition is 21–24 October 2026. Around 3,000 exhibitors over
            110,000 m². This is the strongest pairing we offer: the market, the fair and the
            factories are all inside one city, so you can shortlist on the floor and be on a
            production line the next morning.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">East China Fair · March · Shanghai</span>
          <span
            >The East China Fair (Huajiaohui) is held at the Shanghai New International Expo Centre —
            the 35th edition runs 1–4 March 2027. Jointly hosted by nine East China provinces and
            cities including Zhejiang and Ningbo, weighted towards light industry, garments and
            textiles. Pairs naturally with the textile and household-goods clusters in Zhejiang and
            Jiangsu, a short train ride away.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">Canton Fair · spring and autumn · Guangzhou</span>
          <span
            >The largest and the most crowded, running in spring and autumn sessions. Strong for
            private label and OEM conversations, and it pairs with Guangzhou, Foshan and Dongguan
            factories. Worth knowing: the autumn session overlaps the Yiwu Fair, and a lot of Yiwu
            suppliers are in Guangzhou that week rather than at home.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">Ningbo export consumer goods fair · May · Ningbo</span>
          <span
            >The export hall of the China–CEEC Expo &amp; International Consumer Goods Fair, hosted by
            the Zhejiang provincial government with the Ministry of Commerce. Plan around one thing
            first: it is not annual. The last edition ran 22–25 May 2025 and the organisers list the
            next for May 2027. That edition filled 60,000 m² of the Ningbo International Conference
            and Exhibition Center with around 1,028 exhibitors, and drew overseas buyers from 72
            countries. Categories are household goods, gifts and crafts, appliances, home textiles and
            garments, stationery, outdoor and kitchen-and-bath — close to a map of what this cluster
            actually makes, which is why the factory days afterwards tend to be productive.</span
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="u-mt-6">
        Edition numbers, halls and exact dates belong to the organisers — confirm the current one on
        <a href="https://www.cceecexpo.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cceecexpo.org</a> before you
        book flights. Then
        <a class="link-arrow" href="/destinations/ningbo-hangzhou/">see what a Ningbo factory week looks like</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every fair on this list sits inside a cluster we already work in:
        <a href="/destinations/yiwu/">Yiwu</a> for commodities and small goods,
        <a href="/destinations/guangzhou-dongguan/">Guangzhou and Dongguan</a> for the private-label
        and OEM conversations the Canton Fair is full of, and
        <a href="/destinations/zhejiang/">Zhejiang</a> for the textile and household-goods mills that
        stand behind the East China Fair.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section alternate>
    <div class="container">
      <SectionHead
        eyebrow="How the week runs"
        title="Shortlist on the floor, verify the next day."
        lead="The mistake is treating the fair as the trip. It is the filter; the factory days are the decision."
      />
      <ul class="spec-list">
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">Before you fly</span>
          <span
            >We turn your categories into a target list with hall and district, so the floor is a
            route rather than a wander. Hotels and drivers in a fair city are booked solid weeks
            ahead — this is the step people leave too late.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">On the floor</span>
          <span
            >Collecting prices, MOQs and booth numbers into one structured list, with the same
            five checks applied to whoever is behind the table. Plenty of booths are trading
            companies; that is normal, and it only matters if you do not know.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">The day after</span>
          <span
            >The shortlist goes into factories — usually 30–60 minutes out of the fair city. Two
            visits in a day is the useful number, because the second quote arrives informed by the
            first.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">After you fly home</span>
          <span
            >Every quote normalised to one specification, so comparison is real rather than
            remembered.</span
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="u-mt-6">
        The five checks we run on the floor are the same ones we run on any visit —
        <a href="/about/how-we-vet-factories/">how we vet factories</a> sets them out, and the
        <a href="/tools/factory-visit-scorecard/">factory visit scorecard</a> is where they get
        written down before you forget them. If you would rather have the factory days sequenced
        around the fair for you, that is
        <a href="/services/factory-visit-planning/">visit planning</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <div class="container container--md">
      <SectionHead
        eyebrow="Boundaries"
        title="What we are not."
        lead="Stated plainly, because a fair week attracts a lot of people selling things they should not."
      />
      <ul class="spec-list">
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">Not an exhibitor service</span>
          <span>We do not sell booth space, build stands or register you as an exhibitor.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">Not a commission agent</span>
          <span
            >We are not paid a percentage of your order, and we do not take a cut from the factory.
            That is what lets us tell you a supplier is wrong.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="spec-list__k">Not a date authority</span>
          <span
            >Fair dates, halls and visitor registration belong to the organisers. We plan around
            them; we do not speak for them.</span
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
